    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. quick follow up though—how would the calculation change if we decided to change the salvage value to $10,000 midway through the second year instead? i just want to make sure i understand how to adjust the remaining book value if the estimates shift.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If you recalculated the salvage value during the second year to be $10,000 instead of $5,000, you'd need to slightly adjust the depreciation from that point forward. For the first year, the depreciation remains the same at $11,428.57 for a total accumulated depreciation of $11,428.57 over the year. By the end of the first year, the machine's book value would be $85,000 - $11,428.57 = $73,571.43. By the second year's midpoint, $12,143.15 (half of $11,428.57) would have been already depreciated, making the book value $61,428.28. If you lower the salvage value to $10,000 starting in the second year, you only have two and a half years left (since half of the second year's depreciation has been taken), so the new depreciable value becomes $61,428.28 - $10,000 = $51,428.28. Divided by 2.5 years, the new annual depreciation becomes $20,571.31. So from the second year's midpoint onward, each half-year, you would depreciate by $10,285.",
